WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.875
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There's
a lot the Cloud can

00:00:01.875 --> 00:00:04.320
offer on top of traditional
public sector solutions.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.540
Join us in historic
downtown Austin, Texas,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.300
as we talk to SADA Systems about
how the Cloud enables building

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.710
solutions like Atom.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.682
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:15.910
What does Atom do?

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.660
JOHN BIESAK: So Atom
is a lot more robust.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.220
So in almost the opposite of
how dot maps is very niche

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.650
and kind of has its need and
things that it can address.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:28.280
Atom is going to support
a lot broader, I guess,

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.380
use case within the DOT.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.720
You've got things like asset
management and maintenance

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.810
management, work order
distribution, mobile data

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:36.660
collection.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.327
These are going to
be the core use cases

00:00:38.327 --> 00:00:39.500
that Atom kind of supports.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.333
Some of our main use
cases that we'll tackle

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:43.950
are like bridge and
infrastructure management.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.430
So a lot of the states and
counties in the United States

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.030
they have a budget every year.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.560
They need to essentially upkeep
the bridges and the roads

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.578
and the signs and signals
in inefficient way.

00:00:54.578 --> 00:00:56.870
They have an X amount of
money that they need to spend,

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.618
and they want to track
that kind of whole dollar

00:00:59.618 --> 00:01:01.910
amount against the work that
they're sending out there.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.790
And obviously, when you're
getting into the management

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.100
and upkeep of
infrastructure, especially

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.870
at the bridge and highway level,
safety is a huge concern here.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:11.870
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:13.588
So streamlining these
workflows so people

00:01:13.588 --> 00:01:15.380
don't have to jump
through a bunch of hoops

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.240
to do what they need to
do on an everyday basis.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:17.910
JOHN BIESAK: Correct.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.493
Yeah, it's all about--
and again, this

00:01:19.493 --> 00:01:22.280
is kind of piggybacking
on that bringing material

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.380
design and that
ease of use, but we

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.420
want to make sure
we cater to all ends

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.190
of the spectrum within the DOT.

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:31.250
In the DOT you've got folks that
are very day-to-day users that

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:35.120
are going to be just querying
for very simple things, project

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.367
details, project managers
on a specific project.

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:39.450
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So from
a technical perspective,

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.520
what does Atom look like?

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.950
JOHN BIESAK: Atom essentially
is a cloud-based solution,

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.350
and we're obviously leveraging
Google Cloud for that.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.482
It allows us to do
some things feature

00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:49.190
and functionality wise
that I don't think

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.260
has been quite possible
within the DOT space

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.110
up to this point, things
like offline access,

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:57.920
full native offline access for
our mobile users in the field.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.550
Atom has a fully native iOS
and Android application.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.370
And when you're working
with states that are just

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:08.090
more rural use cases and
assets in more rural areas,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.180
you tend to have a problem where
they can't access their asset

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.240
information that they need,
forms, directions, all

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.500
those types of things.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.440
And having the ability to cache
that data locally on the device

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.080
and push it to the Cloud after
they complete what they need

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.270
to do has been something
that we've received

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.430
a lot of positive feedback on.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.310
Again, some of these states
have very rural areas

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.770
where users are going
out there in the field

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.410
and they need to have
access to asset information,

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.610
they need to have access to
the important information

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.610
that they need to do their
job out in the field.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:42.410
So I think that's a crucial
part in leveraging Google Cloud.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:46.010
And really taking that burden
of infrastructure management

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.890
away from the DOT,
I think is massive.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:52.580
For them to have to
pay to do server manage

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.070
upkeep and things
like that, I think

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.970
those days are behind them.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.180
And I think, because of
Google Cloud's capabilities

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.970
and the security requirements
that they're getting up to,

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.760
the DOT has no reason not to
go on Google Cloud or a cloud

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:05.613
provider.

00:03:05.613 --> 00:03:08.030
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So for a
group that wants to adopt Atom,

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.920
what does that look
like in comparison

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.858
to their existing workflows?

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.650
JOHN BIESAK: Yeah, so
that's a great point.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.525
I think a lot of vendors
make a mistake where

00:03:15.525 --> 00:03:18.320
they want to come in and totally
reinvent what cities are doing

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.990
and states are doing from
a workflow perspective.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:24.590
In reality, the states have
been ironing out these processes

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:25.492
for 20, 30 years.

00:03:25.492 --> 00:03:27.950
You've got folks within the
government that have been there

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.490
for 20, 30 years.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.670
So if you try to come in
there and totally change

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:33.650
what they're doing,
fundamentally, you're

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.692
going to get pushback.

00:03:34.692 --> 00:03:35.900
I mean, you're just going to.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.220
There's folks within
the government that

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.870
have been there a
long time and are

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.510
used to a certain workflow
and a certain process.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:44.540
And I think it's really crucial
that you get those adoption

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.780
rates high quickly to make
sure that you have success

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.615
within the government
with your product.

00:03:49.615 --> 00:03:50.990
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
And all of this

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:53.180
is possible because
of the Cloud?

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:54.270
JOHN BIESAK: Yeah, 100%.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.500
I think that the Cloud
allows for really high level

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.630
collaboration
between these groups.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.520
Again, once you can get
that data to a point

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.770
where it's all sanitized
across these groups,

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:07.970
you can really start sharing and
doing that cross-asset analysis

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.010
that I think all
states want to get to.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.020
It's a process for
them, but the Cloud

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.580
is for sure a way
that that's going

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.790
to enable them to be successful
and to actually start sharing

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.730
that data across departments.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:19.339
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, John, thank

00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:21.006
you so much for coming
in and getting us

00:04:21.006 --> 00:04:22.730
more about public
sector in the Cloud.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:24.397
JOHN BIESAK: Yeah,
appreciate your time.

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:25.710
Thanks so much.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:26.490
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Thanks for watching,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.240
and stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.240
we dive into the technical
side of dot maps.

